---
description: Never nest ternary expressions; use a dictionary or if/switch
alwaysApply: true
---

# No nested ternaries

A single `cond ? a : b` is fine. Nesting another `?` in either branch is not.

Use a dictionary when the input is a closed set of keys. Use `if` / `switch` when the conditions are ordered or overlapping.

```ts
// BAD
const status =
  state.status === 'offline'
    ? 'failed'
    : state.status !== 'live'
      ? 'syncing'
      : reply?.finished
        ? 'completed'
        : reply
          ? 'receiving'
          : 'sent';

// GOOD
if (state.status === 'offline') return 'failed';
if (state.status !== 'live') return 'syncing';
if (!reply) return 'sent';
if (reply.finished) return 'completed';
return 'receiving';

// GOOD — closed set
const tint = { attention: 'warning', failed: 'danger' }[state] ?? 'secondary';
```

Same rule in Swift, TS, and TSX. Optional chaining (`?.`) and nullish (`??`) are not ternaries.
